","Terms with Punjabi translations","Terms with Russian translations","Terms with Spanish translations","Terms with Swedish translations","Terms with Ukrainian translations","Terms with Welsh translations"],"glosses":["Relating to or involving ships or shipping or navigation or seamen."],"tags":["not-comparable","usually"],"id":"b7d81521-ca3b-49cf-920a-ce79f9c49d4b"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[]},"nautical mile":{"id":"6e74a54e-40c4-4198-9fb6-1de57d63501b","word":"nautical mile","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"nautical: a unit of measure equal to 1852 metres","tags":["feminine"],"word":"milha náutica"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"Near-synonym: geographical mile (very close to the same distance)","id":"b4c28981-d16a-420c-98f1-cd4980c6f199"},{"text":"Personnel trenches have been constructed or improved in Tantung (40-05-50N 124-21-30E) and along roads 13 nautical miles (nm) south-southwest of Tantung and 1.5 nm west of the border (Figures 2 and 3).","ref":"1970 May, “Basic Description”, in Chinese Defensive Activity Along the China-North Korea Border, National Photographic Interpretation Center, published 2011, archived from the original on 04 Dec 2023, page 1:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[106,120]],"id":"f1c94c39-d5eb-4a6d-ab19-dc02eee18010"},{"text":"An even more impressive nineteenth-century militarized lighthouse is found on the Taiwanese Wuchiu island, located just 20 nautical miles off the Chinese coast. While much of the surrounding military structure has been demolished, its black tower and high walls painted in camouflage colours stand as reminders of the contested history of this small frontier island.","ref":"2022, Veronica della Dora, Where Light in Darkness Lies: The Story of the Lighthouse, →ISBN, →OCLC:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[123,137]],"id":"a37e8174-240e-484f-9b85-6bb96f2420a3"}],"wikidata":["Q93318"],"senseid":["en:Q93318"],"links":[["minute of arc","minute of arc"],["latitude","latitude"],["meridian","meridian"],["metre","metre"],["feet","feet"],["statute mile","statute mile"]],"related":[{"word":"mul:NM","tags":["alternative"]},{"word":"mul:M","tags":["alternative"]},{"word":"mul:nmi","tags":["alternative"]},{"word":";","tags":["alternative"]},{"word":"mul:nm","tags":["alternative"]}],"synonyms":[{"word":"sea mile"},{"word":"geographical mile"}],"hypernyms":[{"word":"mile"}],"coordinate_terms":[{"word":"land mile"},{"word":";"},{"word":"international mile"},{"word":"statute mile"},{"word":"survey mile"},{"word":"statute mile"}],"categories":["English terms with quotations"],"glosses":["A unit of length corresponding approximately to one minute of arc of latitude along any meridian. By international agreement it is exactly 1,852 metres (approximately 6,076 feet or 1.151 statute mile)."],"id":"a8480386-8de9-49cc-910c-c340ee90a741"},{"categories":["English terms with historical senses"],"links":[["sea mile","sea mile#English"]],"raw_glosses":["(historical) Synonym of sea mile, a historical Scandinavian unit of distance equal to about 4 nmi."],"glosses":["Synonym of sea mile, a historical Scandinavian unit of distance equal to about 4 nmi."],"tags":["historical"],"synonyms":[{"word":"sea mile","extra":"a historical Scandinavian unit of distance equal to about 4 nmi","tags":["synonym","synonym-of"]}],"id":"485832bd-b414-476f-b8fa-a824fe7ada1a"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[]},"nausea":{"id":"e9337439-cc8c-4c37-b42c-2088b2ac0f47","word":"nausea","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"feeling of physical unwellness, usually with the desire to vomit","tags":["feminine"],"word":"náusea"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"feeling of physical unwellness, usually with the desire to vomit","word":"enjoo"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"strong dislike or disgust","tags":["masculine"],"word":"desgosto"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"strong dislike or disgust","tags":["masculine"],"word":"asco"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"strong dislike or disgust","tags":["feminine"],"word":"repulsão"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"He got nausea after two hours of travelling on the plane.","bold_text_offsets":[[7,13]],"type":"example","id":"569feb7d-92ec-40ee-a948-661a1cdfc08d"}],"links":[["digestive system","digestive system"],["vomit","vomit"]],"glosses":["A feeling of illness or discomfort in the digestive system, usually characterized by a strong urge to vomit."],"categories":["English terms with usage examples"],"tags":["uncountable","usually"],"id":"a708f1ba-3ec6-474c-b224-da45e0d700fe"},{"links":[["dislike","dislike"],["disgust","disgust"]],"glosses":["Strong dislike or disgust."],"tags":["uncountable","usually"],"id":"d1b5a8c5-6928-4d6d-ba12-62c598639906"},{"links":[["Motion sickness","motion sickness"]],"glosses":["Motion sickness."],"tags":["uncountable","usually"],"id":"1bc46e0d-4796-4eef-a602-693d995258fd"}],"related":[{"word":"ad nauseam"},{"word":"nautical"}],"synonyms":[]},"nauseous":{"id":"35628619-1775-4a0a-bdf9-92b13c8c80d5","word":"nauseous","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"afflicted with nausea","word":"enjoado"}],"pos":"adj","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"[…] And flattery tipt with nauseous fleer,\nAnd guilty shame, and servile fear,\nEnvy, and cruelty, and pride,\nWill in your tainted heart preside.","ref":"1720, Jonathan Swift, To Stella, visiting me in my sickness:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[27,35]],"id":"b277745c-8fe4-4d5b-a4e8-003c61c30448"},{"text":"[T]he Italians grossness of Conversation is still very offensive & nauseous, tho' long accustomed to it.","ref":"1786 June 27, Hester Thrale Piozzi, Thraliana:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[67,75]],"id":"2b5cc457-9e3c-477c-a702-6b8a25f9a787"},{"text":"And then what proper person can be partial / To all those nauseous epigrams of Martial?","ref":"1819, Lord Byron, Don Juan, section I:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[58,66]],"id":"1b96d59c-64a3-4412-b936-f6ad0414fa82"}],"attestations":[{"date":"from 17th c.","references":[]}],"links":[["nausea","nausea"],["sickening","sickening"],["disgusting","disgusting"]],"categories":["English terms with quotations","Quotation templates to be cleaned"],"glosses":["Causing nausea; sickening or disgusting."],"id":"2a9f4e3f-dcf4-4821-9090-cd24b96dfbdf"},{"attestations":[{"date":"17th c.","references":[]}],"categories":["English terms with obsolete senses"],"links":[["sickly","sickly"],["squeamish","squeamish"]],"raw_glosses":["(obsolete) Inclined to nausea; sickly, squeamish."],"glosses":["Inclined to nausea; sickly, squeamish."],"tags":["obsolete"],"id":"fc13b120-6be5-4f5f-afa1-2416fbb282d3"},{"examples":[{"text":"After he had scarcely eaten enough, he felt nauseous; but nausea ceased as soon as he stopped eating entirely, …","ref":"1848, Samuel Hahnemann, The Chronic Diseases, Their Specific Nature and Their Homeopathic Treatment: Antipsoric Remedies, Volume 2:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[44,52]],"id":"d5f1449b-df8a-4d3e-8e85-a8187c403cfb"},{"text":"[…] during stretching the patient felt nauseous …","ref":"1878, The North American Journal of Homeopathy, Volume 27:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[39,47]],"id":"d1f4367c-e771-47ac-b9e6-6f10f7045dc3"},{"text":"Is it a myth that you shouldn't drink alcohol while taking antibiotics? I often do and haven't felt remotely nauseous.","ref":"2010 September 4, Tom Smith, The Guardian:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[109,117]],"id":"627b8104-945d-4f8f-acfd-d3f3bda88389"}],"attestations":[{"date":"from 19th c.","references":[]}],"categories":["English proscribed terms","English terms with quotations","Quotation templates to be cleaned"],"links":[["sick","sick"]],"raw_glosses":["(proscribed) Afflicted with nausea; sick."],"glosses":["Afflicted with nausea; sick."],"tags":["proscribed"],"id":"023387d6-c20b-4285-a1e8-dde0dd433a9e"}],"related":[{"english":"afflicted with nausea","translation":"afflicted with nausea","word":"nauseated"},{"word":"queasy"}],"synonyms":[{"english":"causing disgust rather than nausea","translation":"causing disgust rather than nausea","word":"nauseating"}]},"nauseating":{"id":"87f69e64-83e1-43c5-be2d-74c770d937fd","word":"nauseating","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"causing disgust, revulsion or loathing","word":"nauseante"}],"pos":"adj","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"But in the mean time […] it looks all ſo like ſubterfuge and inveagling; it is ſo nauſeating and teadious a task, that no man thinks he ovvs the Author ſo much ſervice as to find out the reaſon of his ovvn Categoricalneſs for him.","ref":"1672, [Andrew Marvell], The Rehearsal Transpros’d: Or, Animadversions upon a Late Book, Entituled, A Preface, Shewing what Grounds there are of Fears and Jealousies of Popery, London: [s.n.], →OCLC, pages 57–58:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[82,92]],"id":"1288a79f-deee-4131-9e42-a37fb8ab1daa"},{"text":"They gathered soberly in the farthest recess of the ward and gossiped about him in malicious, offended undertones, rebelling against his presence as a ghastly imposition and resenting him malevolently for the nauseating truth of which he was bright reminder.","ref":"1961 November 10, Joseph Heller, “The Soldier in White”, in Catch-22 […], New York, N.Y.: Simon and Schuster, →OCLC, page 169:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[209,219]],"id":"3022643b-5e5d-49ea-a934-6b125f11c621"}],"links":[["nausea","nausea"],["disgusting","disgusting"],["revolting","revolting"]],"synonyms":[{"word":"nauseous"}],"glosses":["Causing a feeling of nausea; disgusting and revolting."],"categories":["English terms with quotations"],"id":"c1711b5c-0678-4de3-b19c-72fe8e1d82e2"}],"related":[{"word":"nauseated"}],"synonyms":[]},"naught":{"id":"9a