of the Beades:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[26,33]],"id":"d3ed9ad8-cb36-47b1-82e7-1c1d61ef757c"},{"text":"[…]How far that little candle throws his beams!\nSo shines a good deed in a naughty world.","ref":"c. 1596–1598 (date written), William Shakespeare, “The Merchant of Venice”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act V, scene i]:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[75,82]],"id":"39e78666-a97d-429b-ac26-0f03571942c5"},{"text":"A wicked doer giveth heed to false lips; and a liar giveth ear to a naughty tongue.","ref":"1611, The Holy Bible, […] (King James Version), London: […] Robert Barker, […], →OCLC, Proverbs 17:4:","type":"quotation","id":"1674ac99-fcac-448b-8a33-7310fccc6d50"},{"text":"Wholesome meats to a vitiated stomack differ little or nothing from unwholesome; and best books to a naughty mind are not unappliable to occasions of evill.","ref":"1644, John Milton, Areopagitica; a Speech of Mr. John Milton for the Liberty of Unlicenc’d Printing, to the Parlament of England, London: [s.n.], →OCLC:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[101,108]],"id":"ce04c001-58f7-4c37-aef0-61c2f8e91b60"},{"text":"Meowky as naughty folks","ref":"1983, Namco, Mappy:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[10,17]],"id":"34ce8770-dace-49d1-a6c1-1f42265b826b"}],"attestations":[{"date":"from 15th c.","references":[]}],"categories":["English terms with archaic senses","English terms with quotations","English terms with rare senses","Quotation templates to be cleaned"],"links":[["Evil","evil"],["wicked","wicked"],["reprehensible","reprehensible"]],"synonyms":[{"word":"baneful"},{"word":"nithing"},{"word":"sordid"},{"word":"abandoned","source":"Thesaurus:evil"},{"word":"arrant","source":"Thesaurus:evil","tags":["dated"]},{"word":"bad","source":"Thesaurus:evil"},{"word":"bad apple","source":"Thesaurus:evil"},{"word":"bad seed","source":"Thesaurus:e